# CONTROL PLUGIN - ipmi/bmc control
#
#        12 defined methods are mandatory which return <True|False>,message:
#        - power_on
#        - power_off
#        - power_reset
#        - power_cycle
#        - power_status
#        - identify
#        - no_identify
#        - sel_list
#        - sel_clear
#        - boot_bios      arm a one-shot boot into BIOS setup and reset into it
#        - boot_status    read the current boot override back
#        - boot_clear     disarm the boot override without a reset
#
#        The boot methods are Redfish only: the IPMI boot flags are not
#        reliable, so default.py refuses them with the reason rather than trying.

# Plugin selection based on the following search path and priority:
#
# 1 node name                 e.g. plugins/control/node001.py
# 2 group name                e.g. plugins/control/compute.py
# 3 manufacturer              e.g. plugins/control/dell.py
# 4 redfish                   e.g. plugins/control/redfish.py
# 5 default                   e.g. plugins/control/default.py
#
# Steps 1 to 4 are each narrowed by the node's model, which is offered first:
#
#   <name><model>.py    e.g. plugins/control/dellpoweredger650.py
#   <name>/<model>.py   e.g. plugins/control/dell/poweredger650.py
#   <name>/default.py   e.g. plugins/control/dell/default.py
#   <name>.py           e.g. plugins/control/dell.py
#
# so hardware that differs within one vendor gets its own file without the vendor
# file having to know about it.
#
# No vendor file ships here. The redfish plugin is offered only with evidence that
# the BMC speaks Redfish (a redfishsetup or a redfish inventory snapshot); a vendor
# file that inherits it is selected by manufacturer regardless, and costs every
# node of that vendor without Redfish a connect timeout before the ipmitool
# fallback. Vendor behaviour belongs in plugins/redfish/<vendor>.py.

# The manufacturer and the model are DERIVED, from the node's nodeinventory row -
# there is no column for an administrator to set. Where both an inband and a
# redfish snapshot exist the redfish one wins, because it is the BMC's own answer
# and it exists before a node has ever been provisioned.
#
# Both are normalised into a filename: lowercased, non-alphanumerics dropped, and
# for the manufacturer only the first word is kept, since vendor strings carry a
# company suffix. So:
#
#   'Dell Inc.'                  -> dell
#   'VMware, Inc.'               -> vmware
#   'Supermicro'                 -> supermicro
#   'PowerEdge R650'             -> poweredger650
#
# Note what first-word costs: a board reporting 'Hewlett Packard Enterprise'
# resolves to 'hewlett', not 'hpe'. Name the file after what the hardware actually
# reports rather than after what the vendor is called.

# Step 4, the vendor-neutral redfish plugin, is only offered where there is
# evidence the BMC speaks Redfish - a redfishsetup assigned to the node or its
# group, or an inventory snapshot that came from Redfish. Without that evidence a
# node falls through to default.py exactly as it always did. The reason is cost:
# a redfish plugin tries Redfish and falls back to ipmitool, so offering it to a
# BMC that does not answer buys a connect timeout per node.

# redfish.py holds everything standards-based. A vendor file subclasses it and
# overrides only what that vendor does differently - dell.py overrides nothing
# today, and exists as the name the search path resolves and as the place Dell
# behaviour goes when a board needs it.
